is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF default route origination on r1 (FRR daemon). Mechanism: Access switches and distribution/core switches have full OSPF adjacency and exchange internal routes, so inter-office traffic works. However, when hosts try to reach the Internet (e.g., 203.0.113.1), their first-hop DSW replies “Destination Net Unreachable” because it has no route to external networks and no OSPF-learned default. This is evidenced by PC pings failing with ICMP Net Unreachable sourced from 10.1.0.2 (dswa1 SVI) and by the absence of 0.0.0.0/0 in dswa1/csw1/csw2 routing tables. Localization: r1 FRR OSPF config has “default-information originate” but r1’s RIB has no default route, so OSPF doesn’t inject a default into the fabric. On r1, ip route shows no default; only connected 203.0.113.0/30 and 203.0.113.4/30. r1 itself can ping ISP peers (203.0.113.1 and .5) because those are directly connected, confirming the problem is not the ISP links but the lack of default propagation. Root cause: r1 is missing a default route in its RIB, so OSPF does not originate a default toward the LAN. Corrective fix: Install a default route on r1 to the ISP(s), e.g., “ip route add 0.0.0.0/0 via 203.0.113.1” and optionally a secondary via 203.0.113.5 with higher metric, or change FRR to “default-information originate always.” After adding the default, OSPF will advertise 0/0, DSWs/CSWs will install it, and Internet-bound traffic from PCs will forward to r1 and out to the ISPs. === END ===